Package: videonsole
Version: @VERSION@-1
Architecture: @ARCH@
Maintainer: Piotr Lewandowski <piotr.petexy@gmail.com>
Section: video
Priority: optional
Depends: @SHLIB_DEPENDS@, libxkbcommon0, libegl1
Recommends: xdg-desktop-portal
Suggests: libvulkan1, va-driver-all
Installed-Size: @INSTALLED_SIZE@
Homepage: https://github.com/Petexy/videonsole
Description: a film browser and player in the LineXinBar design language
 Shown as Videos. A folder is a wall of films, each card wearing a frame out of
 the film itself and how long it runs. Those frames are written to the same
 thumbnail cache every other program on the machine shares, so a folder browsed
 here is a folder with pictures on it everywhere else too.
 .
 Playing one fills the screen with the film. The controls come up when anything
 is pressed and go away four seconds later, and the film grows to take the whole
 screen when they do — nothing is ever drawn over the picture except its
 subtitles. Left and right move through the film, ten seconds a press and
 further while held; a pad's triggers scan through it; up and down are the
 volume.
 .
 Where you left off is remembered and offered again the next time that film is
 opened, and a film watched to its end is forgotten rather than remembered at
 its credits. Subtitles come from inside the film or from a file beside it.
 .
 It is an ordinary Wayland application, drawn in the LineXinBar design language
 and driven from a controller, a keyboard and a pointer at once. It runs under
 GNOME or Plasma as readily as under the shell it was made for.
 .
 libxkbcommon0 and libegl1 are named here because they are opened by name at
 run time rather than linked, so nothing that reads the executable can find
 them. The ffmpeg libraries are linked outright and so are found for
 themselves. libvulkan1 is only suggested: with a Vulkan driver present this
 draws through it, and without one it falls back to EGL. va-driver-all is what
 lets a film decode on the graphics card rather than the processor, which is
 the difference between a 4K film playing and not.
